{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310857,"nonce":"imhzqUp3YOqe6dU3hVFJZk//9Is=","title":"Phase 4b (Linear dispatching directly through QTensor.matmulF32, skipping the F32 dequant) is wired end-to-end but blocked by a JIT ...","message":"**Status: Kernel + plumbing landed and unit-tested; end-to-end blocked\nby a JIT instability that is independent of the Phase 4b code itself.**\n\n\n- **Runtime kernel** `runtime/src/quant.rs::rayzor_tensor_matmul_qt_t_f32`\n  computes `Y[B, N] = X[B, K] × Wq[N, K].T` directly over a Q4_K_M\n  QTensor with no F32 dequant. Dequants one Wq row at a time into a\n  K-sized scratch buffer; reuses it across the batch.\n- **Runtime test** `matmul_qt_t_f32_matches_dequant_then_matmul_t`\n  cross-checks the fused kernel against the dequant-then-matmul_t path\n  on a 3-batch × 256-out × 512-in fixture. Matches to within 1e-3 over\n  all entries.\n- **Haxe binding** `rayzor.ds.QTensor.matmulXTQ(x:Tensor):Tensor` —\n  i.e. `y = x @ self.T`. Linear's natural matmul.\n- **Linear** has a new `qweight:Null\u003cQTensor\u003e` field and a\n  `Linear.fromQuant(qw, ?bias, ?paramName)` factory. `forward`\n  dispatches through `qweight.matmulXTQ(x)` when the quant slot is set.\n  `parameters()` skips the F32 entry for quantised layers (callers\n  that need to enumerate quantised weights will get a follow-up\n  `quantParameters()` accessor).\n- **NamedTensorMap** carries a parallel `qtensorByName:StringMap\u003cQTensor\u003e`\n  channel; `setQuant` / `getQuant` / `isQuantised` round it out.\n- **GGUFLoader.populateTensor** stores Q4_K_M weights via `setQuant`\n  (no `.dequant()` materialised); `decodeQ4KMRaw` is the new helper.\n  `decodeQ4KM` kept as a legacy F32-dequant fallback.\n- **LlamaArch.buildLinear** consults `weights.isQuantised(name)` and\n  picks `Linear.fromQuant` when applicable; `takeWeightOrDequant`\n  covers the F32-only consumers (embedding, RMSNorm scale).\n- **tiny-transformer**: still PASSES on the F32 path (untouched code path).\n- **128/128 haxe regression** green. **709/709 compiler lib tests**\n  green. **8/8 quant runtime tests** green (serial).\n\n\nEnd-to-end on llama-chat (Llama-3 8B, ~70 transitive imports) SIGILLs\nwith `udf #0xc11f` inside JIT'd code somewhere between\n`ArchRegistry.build()` returning a builder and `LlamaArch.build()`\nstarting. Adding/removing `Sys.println` markers shifts the crash\nlocation — sometimes `Array\u003cArchEntry\u003e[i]` indexed access SIGILLs,\nsometimes `for (e in entries)` works in build() but identical iteration\ninside get() crashes, sometimes get() returns null even when entries\nclearly contain \"llama\".\n\nThis is **JIT/cross-context cache instability**, not a bug in the Phase\n4b code. The pattern matches the symptom family in\n[[bugs_anon_field_scramble]] and the various\n`bugs_cross_module_dispatch_complexity` regressions — class field\noffsets / array element access getting scrambled by the order in which\nspecific functions get JIT-compiled under a large import graph.\n\n\n```bash\nrm -rf nue/examples/llama-chat/.rayzor\ncd nue/examples/llama-chat\nrayzor run Main.hx -- \u003cany GGUF that's \u003e2 GiB\u003e\n```\n\nThe smaller `tiny-transformer` example (~30 imports) doesn't trigger\nthe issue. Increasing the import graph or making Linear hold the new\n`Null\u003cQTensor\u003e` field both push the system into the bad state.\n\n\n1. Investigate whether `NamedTensorMap`'s new field (`qtensorByName`)\n   shifted class field indices in a way that the BLADE cache loads\n   back inconsistently. Try renaming or putting the new field first.\n2. Check whether `Linear.weight` becoming `Null\u003cTensor\u003e` affected\n   field-layout indexing across cross-context compilations. Try\n   keeping `weight:Tensor` non-null and using a sentinel zero-shape\n   tensor for the quantised case instead.\n3. Add a `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` run, capture every `Array\u003c*\u003e.get`\n   / `arrayIterator.next` symbol address, and check if any get\n   re-resolved between compilations.\n4. The `for-in` iterator vs indexed access asymmetry is suggestive —\n   investigate whether `Array.iterator()` and `ArrayIterator.next()`\n   are reading different field-offset state than `Array.__get`.\n\n\n- [[bugs_q4_km_layout_and_linear_convention]] — the layout fix that\n  enabled this work. Cleanly landed and verified, independent of\n  Phase 4b plumbing.\n- [[bugs_anon_field_scramble]] — same shape of bug; field-index\n  consistency across compilation contexts.\n- [[bugs_cross_module_dispatch_complexity]] — multi-cause family of\n  JIT-related regressions surfaced by large import graphs.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_phase4b_qtensor_linear` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}